1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,500
Sunday morning.

2
00:00:01,774 --> 00:00:04,365
The seventh of August, 1892.

3
00:00:04,705 --> 00:00:09,897
Alice Russell, a family friend, later
testified that she found Lizzie Borden at

4
00:00:09,897 --> 00:00:12,402
the kitchen stove, holding a dress.

5
00:00:12,742 --> 00:00:14,661
Lizzie said she was going to burn it.

6
00:00:15,021 --> 00:00:17,247
It was covered with paint, she said.

7
00:00:17,587 --> 00:00:20,177
Three days earlier, in that same house,

8
00:00:20,177 --> 00:00:23,227
Lizzie's father and
stepmother had been killed.

9
00:00:23,567 --> 00:00:26,558
Ten months later, a jury
found her not guilty.

10
00:00:26,898 --> 00:00:28,696
No one else was ever charged.

11
00:00:29,036 --> 00:00:31,356
So who killed Andrew and Abby Borden?

12
00:00:34,096 --> 00:00:37,263
In 1892, Fall River was a mill city.

13
00:00:37,623 --> 00:00:39,315
People called it Spindle City.

14
00:00:39,655 --> 00:00:43,618
And Andrew Borden had made himself
a wealthy man, in business,

15
00:00:43,618 --> 00:00:45,192
property and banking.

16
00:00:45,532 --> 00:00:49,787
He lived at 92 Second Street,
with his second wife, Abby.

17
00:00:50,147 --> 00:00:52,516
They had married in 1865.

18
00:00:52,856 --> 00:00:55,877
His two daughters from his
first marriage lived there too.

19
00:00:56,237 --> 00:00:57,365
Emma, the elder.

20
00:00:57,725 --> 00:00:59,839
And Lizzie, who was thirty-two.

21
00:01:00,178 --> 00:01:03,254
So did the family's maid,
Bridget Sullivan.

22
00:01:03,614 --> 00:01:05,135
The sisters called her Maggie.

23
00:01:05,475 --> 00:01:07,975
There was friction in
the house, over property.

24
00:01:08,315 --> 00:01:12,973
In 1887, Andrew bought a share of
a house for Abby's half-sister,

25
00:01:12,973 --> 00:01:15,072
and put it in Abby's name.

26
00:01:15,432 --> 00:01:19,024
Emma later testified that it
caused trouble in the family.

27
00:01:19,364 --> 00:01:23,146
At the inquest, Lizzie said she
had stopped calling Abby mother.

28
00:01:23,506 --> 00:01:26,777
Asked why, she said it
was because she wanted to.

29
00:01:27,117 --> 00:01:30,844
But Bridget testified that she
never saw the two of them quarrel.

30
00:01:31,184 --> 00:01:35,793
In the days before the killings, Andrew
and Abby were sick to their stomachs.

31
00:01:36,133 --> 00:01:38,879
Abby went to the family
doctor, Seabury Bowen,

32
00:01:38,879 --> 00:01:41,888
and told him she was afraid
they had been poisoned.

33
00:01:42,248 --> 00:01:43,248
He was sceptical.

34
00:01:43,561 --> 00:01:48,004
The day before the killings, according
to a drugstore clerk named Eli Bence,

35
00:01:48,004 --> 00:01:52,393
a woman he identified as Lizzie tried
to buy prussic acid, a deadly poison.

36
00:01:52,733 --> 00:01:55,421
The jury at her trial
would never hear his story.

37
00:01:55,761 --> 00:01:59,269
That day, Russell testified,
Lizzie came to see her.

38
00:01:59,609 --> 00:02:04,537
Russell testified that Lizzie told her: I
feel afraid something is going to happen.

39
00:02:04,877 --> 00:02:10,003
And Lizzie's uncle, John Morse, arrived
unexpectedly, and stayed the night.

40
00:02:10,343 --> 00:02:13,552
Thursday, the fourth of
August, was a hot day.

41
00:02:13,892 --> 00:02:18,977
Emma was away, visiting friends in
Fairhaven, about fifteen miles off.

42
00:02:19,317 --> 00:02:20,699
John Morse went out.

43
00:02:21,059 --> 00:02:23,636
He was visiting relatives
when the killings happened.

44
00:02:23,976 --> 00:02:27,495
Andrew left at about nine,
to do business downtown.

45
00:02:27,835 --> 00:02:32,522
Abby asked Bridget to wash the windows,
and Bridget spent much of the morning

46
00:02:32,522 --> 00:02:33,522
outside.

47
00:02:33,653 --> 00:02:38,223
At about half past nine, Abby went
upstairs to tidy the guest room.

48
00:02:38,563 --> 00:02:41,554
Within roughly the next hour,
she was killed there.

49
00:02:41,894 --> 00:02:44,687
Shortly before eleven, Andrew came home.

50
00:02:45,027 --> 00:02:47,117
The front door was locked three ways.

51
00:02:47,477 --> 00:02:49,711
Bridget had to unlock it to let him in.

52
00:02:50,051 --> 00:02:54,310
Bridget testified that as she fumbled
with the locks, she said, pshaw,

53
00:02:54,310 --> 00:02:56,495
and heard Lizzie laugh, upstairs.

54
00:02:56,855 --> 00:02:58,004
She did not see her.

55
00:02:58,344 --> 00:03:00,712
Andrew lay down on the
sofa in the sitting room.

56
00:03:01,072 --> 00:03:03,614
Bridget went up to her attic room to rest.

57
00:03:03,974 --> 00:03:06,982
By her clock, she
testified, it was eleven.

58
00:03:07,322 --> 00:03:10,576
A few minutes after eleven,
Lizzie called up to her.

59
00:03:10,936 --> 00:03:13,046
Her father was dead, she said.

60
00:03:13,406 --> 00:03:15,243
Somebody had come in and killed him.

61
00:03:15,583 --> 00:03:18,039
A neighbour, Adelaide Churchill, came.

62
00:03:18,399 --> 00:03:20,033
So did the family doctor.

63
00:03:20,374 --> 00:03:22,493
Lizzie said Abby had gone out.

64
00:03:22,853 --> 00:03:28,033
A messenger had brought a note, she said,
asking her to visit someone who was sick.

65
00:03:28,373 --> 00:03:31,977
Neither the note, nor the
messenger, was ever found.

66
00:03:32,317 --> 00:03:37,512
Then, Bridget testified, Lizzie said she
was almost sure she had heard Abby come

67
00:03:37,512 --> 00:03:38,512
back in.

68
00:03:38,698 --> 00:03:42,599
Bridget would not go upstairs alone.
Mrs. Churchill went with her.

69
00:03:42,959 --> 00:03:44,955
They found Abby in the guest room.

70
00:03:45,295 --> 00:03:48,266
Medical evidence showed
that Abby had died first,

71
00:03:48,266 --> 00:03:50,912
roughly an hour or more
before her husband.

72
00:03:51,252 --> 00:03:52,376
So where was Lizzie?

73
00:03:52,716 --> 00:03:56,594
She told the neighbour and the doctor
that she had gone out to the barn,

74
00:03:56,594 --> 00:03:57,887
for a piece of iron.

75
00:03:58,227 --> 00:04:02,481
At the inquest, she said she spent
fifteen or twenty minutes up in the loft,

76
00:04:02,481 --> 00:04:05,075
looking for lead sinkers
for a fishing trip.

77
00:04:05,435 --> 00:04:06,991
And she ate some pears.

78
00:04:07,331 --> 00:04:11,690
Police said the loft was stifling hot,
and that they found no footprints there to

79
00:04:11,690 --> 00:04:12,893
back up her story.

80
00:04:13,233 --> 00:04:17,496
But a defence witness, an ice
cream peddler named Hyman Lubinsky,

81
00:04:17,496 --> 00:04:22,660
testified that shortly after eleven he
saw a woman walk from the barn toward the

82
00:04:22,660 --> 00:04:23,660
house.

83
00:04:23,720 --> 00:04:26,172
And at the inquest, her answers shifted.

84
00:04:26,532 --> 00:04:28,482
She said she was on the stairs.

85
00:04:28,842 --> 00:04:29,943
Then in the kitchen.

86
00:04:30,303 --> 00:04:31,952
Then perhaps the dining room.

87
00:04:32,292 --> 00:04:34,789
In the basement, police
found a hatchet head.

88
00:04:35,149 --> 00:04:36,925
Its handle had been broken off.

89
00:04:37,264 --> 00:04:40,244
The prosecution could never
prove it was the murder weapon.

90
00:04:40,584 --> 00:04:44,373
The inquest ran from the ninth
to the eleventh of August.

91
00:04:44,733 --> 00:04:46,568
Lizzie testified without a lawyer.

92
00:04:46,909 --> 00:04:48,742
On the eleventh, she was arrested.

93
00:04:49,102 --> 00:04:53,461
The next day she pleaded not guilty,
and she was taken to the jail in Taunton.

94
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:56,679
Which brings us back to that
Sunday morning, in the kitchen.

95
00:04:57,019 --> 00:05:00,148
Russell testified that Lizzie
was at the stove with a dress,

96
00:05:00,148 --> 00:05:01,855
and that Emma was in the room.

97
00:05:02,195 --> 00:05:04,713
Lizzie said she was going
to burn this old thing up.

98
00:05:05,073 --> 00:05:06,525
It was covered with paint.

99
00:05:06,865 --> 00:05:10,869
Russell said she told Lizzie she
would not let anyone see her do that.

100
00:05:11,209 --> 00:05:15,016
But Russell also testified that
she saw no blood on the dress,

101
00:05:15,016 --> 00:05:17,816
and never actually saw
it go into the stove.

102
00:05:18,156 --> 00:05:22,961
Emma testified that it was an old dress,
stained with paint soon after it was made

103
00:05:22,961 --> 00:05:23,961
that May.

104
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:28,876
When Lizzie said she would burn it,
Emma's answer, she said, was: do it.

105
00:05:29,216 --> 00:05:33,361
Russell testified that she had not
mentioned the dress at the early hearings.

106
00:05:33,701 --> 00:05:36,944
And at first, the grand
jury did not indict Lizzie.

107
00:05:37,284 --> 00:05:42,265
Then, on the first of December, Russell
went back and told them about the dress.

108
00:05:42,625 --> 00:05:45,352
The next day, Lizzie
was indicted for murder.

109
00:05:45,692 --> 00:05:49,201
Prosecutors said the dress was
burned to cover up the crime.

110
00:05:49,561 --> 00:05:53,833
The defence said it was burned in
broad daylight, in front of witnesses.

111
00:05:54,173 --> 00:05:58,475
The trial opened in New Bedford
on the fifth of June, 1893.

112
00:05:58,815 --> 00:06:02,122
Newspapers all over the country,
and beyond, followed it.

113
00:06:02,462 --> 00:06:04,749
Three judges sat on the case.

114
00:06:05,109 --> 00:06:06,502
The jury was twelve men.

115
00:06:06,843 --> 00:06:11,731
Lizzie's lead lawyer was George Robinson,
a former governor of Massachusetts.

116
00:06:12,071 --> 00:06:16,389
And as governor, Robinson had
appointed one of the three judges.

117
00:06:16,729 --> 00:06:21,118
One of the prosecutors, William Moody,
would later sit on the United States

118
00:06:21,118 --> 00:06:22,202
Supreme Court.

119
00:06:22,542 --> 00:06:26,642
Two things never reached the jury:
Lizzie's inquest testimony,

120
00:06:26,642 --> 00:06:29,356
and the drugstore clerk's poison story.

121
00:06:29,716 --> 00:06:31,421
The judges kept both out.

122
00:06:31,761 --> 00:06:33,431
Lizzie never took the stand.

123
00:06:33,771 --> 00:06:38,101
In his charge to the jury, Justice Dewey
stressed the presumption of innocence,

124
00:06:38,101 --> 00:06:40,087
and the gaps in the state's case.

125
00:06:40,447 --> 00:06:43,193
One newspaper said it was
a plea for the innocent.

126
00:06:43,533 --> 00:06:46,372
On the twentieth of June,
the jury went out.

127
00:06:46,712 --> 00:06:48,732
Accounts differ on how long they took.

128
00:06:49,092 --> 00:06:50,201
About an hour.

129
00:06:50,561 --> 00:06:52,175
Some say an hour and a half.

130
00:06:52,515 --> 00:06:53,515
Not guilty.

131
00:06:55,115 --> 00:06:57,983
Lizzie and Emma inherited
their father's fortune.

132
00:06:58,323 --> 00:07:02,087
They bought a big house in one of
the best neighbourhoods in town,

133
00:07:02,087 --> 00:07:04,021
and Lizzie named it Maplecroft.

134
00:07:04,361 --> 00:07:06,358
She began calling herself Lizbeth.

135
00:07:06,698 --> 00:07:09,647
But much of Fall River shunned
her for the rest of her life.

136
00:07:09,987 --> 00:07:12,611
In 1905, Emma moved out.

137
00:07:12,971 --> 00:07:14,763
The sisters never reconciled.

138
00:07:15,102 --> 00:07:18,467
Lizzie died on the first of June, 1927.

139
00:07:18,827 --> 00:07:20,543
Emma died nine days later.

140
00:07:20,883 --> 00:07:24,584
In her will, Lizzie left money
to the Animal Rescue League.

141
00:07:24,924 --> 00:07:27,244
So who killed Andrew and Abby Borden?

142
00:07:27,584 --> 00:07:29,969
Prosecutors argued that it was Lizzie.

143
00:07:30,329 --> 00:07:31,393
She was at home.

144
00:07:31,753 --> 00:07:33,077
She burned a dress.

145
00:07:33,437 --> 00:07:37,683
A clerk said she had tried to buy poison,
though the jury never heard that.

146
00:07:38,023 --> 00:07:42,305
Historians have argued that her class,
and the fact that she was a woman,

147
00:07:42,305 --> 00:07:44,744
help explain why she was not convicted.

148
00:07:45,084 --> 00:07:48,047
But no blood was found on
her, no weapon was proven,

149
00:07:48,047 --> 00:07:50,959
and the jury that heard
the evidence acquitted her.

150
00:07:51,299 --> 00:07:55,446
Writers have pointed at others:
at Bridget, at John Morse, at Emma,

151
00:07:55,446 --> 00:07:57,094
at an unknown intruder.

152
00:07:57,434 --> 00:07:59,953
There is no direct
evidence against Bridget.

153
00:08:00,313 --> 00:08:01,500
Morse had an alibi.

154
00:08:01,860 --> 00:08:03,264
Emma was miles away.

155
00:08:03,604 --> 00:08:07,341
In 2012, the journals of
Lizzie's own lawyer surfaced.

156
00:08:07,701 --> 00:08:11,731
They had sat for years in his
attic, in a Victorian bathtub.

157
00:08:12,072 --> 00:08:13,283
They did not solve it.

158
00:08:13,643 --> 00:08:16,441
Officially, the case is still unsolved.

159
00:08:16,781 --> 00:08:21,552
Today, the house on Second Street is
a museum, and a bed and breakfast.

160
00:08:21,912 --> 00:08:23,172
You can stay the night.

161
00:08:23,511 --> 00:08:28,502
This autumn, a Netflix drama sent Lizzie
Borden to number one all over again.

162
00:08:28,862 --> 00:08:30,856
This film has kept to the record.

163
00:08:31,196 --> 00:08:32,647
Lizzie Borden was acquitted.

164
00:08:33,007 --> 00:08:34,805
No one else was ever charged.

165
00:08:35,165 --> 00:08:39,835
And more than a hundred and thirty
years later, the question is still open.
